## Counterexample at M

A finding targets a first PR head whose code.py says X = 1. Before that replay succeeds, the author pushes a second recorded head changing X to 2. Both finding/head pairs are due according to warrant.review.to_replay. warrant.review.replay (lines 145-151) silently skips the first head because its code_tree differs from the current checkout. It runs only the second head's test. After that observation is present, the runner returns successfully with empty stdout, although to_replay still lists the first head. There is no jail denial, timeout or test-application failure in this case.

The waker treats a successful return without denied findings as complete (waker.py:643-648), and cover (700-708) marks the older failed replay covered_by the new branch head, without checking which finding/head pairs were actually observed. wake_after_replay (1315-1326) then wakes with gap=None. Thus the failure at the original head has neither a checker observation nor an incomplete-replay disclosure. T explicitly claims that a complete later replay covers earlier failed ones; that is false when their code differs. D also requires the finding test replayed at each head. The denied-test residual in T does not cover this case.

The unsigned controls run the same test against the old and fixed code: reproduces at X=1, does_not_reproduce at X=2. Existing R02 tests enumerate both pairs but do not test that both are executed through the waker.

## Probe boundary

The script below executes the actual replay function and run_test on a local fixture repository. Its signer is a public-only SimpleNamespace with no seed or signing method; repo.file is intercepted and only collects unsigned proposed attest records. The next report includes the one collected observation in memory, modeling its successful publication. The waker's publication boundary is simulated with _replay return values; its actual replay, cover, memo and wake_after_replay methods are used. No checker key is read, no replay attest is signed, no real jail, GitHub push or network delivery is exercised. Fixture input records use the deterministic test actors from tests/world.py. All fixture and scratch files are outside the review worktree.

A repair must track coverage by finding/head pair and replay missing pairs at their own commits (or preserve and disclose them as unobserved, with the obligation's scope explicitly resolved). Merely suppressing cover still leaves the per-head execution gap. A regression should change code between the targeted and successor heads and verify that the old reproducing result is obtained, rather than accepting a successful process exit or empty output as full coverage.

